UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Calming strategies: breathing and stretching', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA friend breathes in fast and loudly when upset. Write the steps you would tell them for belly breathing and for one safe stretch, including when to stop.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSteps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nTeach one breathing strategy and one gentle stretch to a family member. Write the steps in numbered order, record their feeling number before and after, and add one adjustment for someone who cannot do the stretch standing up.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-SMI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Teach one breathing strategy and one gentle stretch to a family member. Write the steps in numbered order, record their feeling number before and after, and add one adjustment for someone who cannot do the stretch standi Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using belly breathing on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming.. Correction: Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating belly breathing on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with belly breathing, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about belly breathing with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for belly breathing. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for belly breathing. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming"],"question":"A friend breathes in fast and loudly when upset. Write the steps you would tell them for belly breathing and for one safe stretch, including when to stop. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for belly breathing; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly","Careful use of a gentle stretch and explain why it matt with clear evidence","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming","Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with belly breathing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a gentle stretch and explain why it matt, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply belly breathing to new material and name the a gentle stretch and explain why it matt used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming","Name a gentle stretch and explain why it matt only, without linking it to belly breathing"],"question":"Which response best applies belly breathing with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on belly breathing. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming","Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for belly breathing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with belly breathing","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about belly breathing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: five-finger breathing","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about belly breathing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for belly breathing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Teach one breathing strategy and one gentle stretch to a family member","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming","Name a gentle stretch and explain why it matt only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies belly breathing to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about belly breathing with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating belly breathing on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using belly breathing on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for belly breathing. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with belly breathing, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming.. Correction: Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise belly breathing.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use belly breathing with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a gentle stretch and explain why it matters.","I can practise slow and steady independently on a new prompt.","I can notice stopping safely needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about belly breathing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you slide up a finger and out slowly as you slide down. After one hand, do three slow backwards shoulder rolls. Feeling rating before: 4 out of 5 frustrated. After: 2.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA friend breathes in fast and loudly when upset. Write the steps you would tell them for belly breathing and for one safe stretch, including when to stop.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSteps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.","explain":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Slow belly breathing in and out.","caption":"Slow and steady.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">In</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">belly rises, slow nose breath</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">slow breath through mouth</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about belly breathing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you slide up a finger and out slowly as you slide down. After one hand, do three slow backwards shoulder rolls. Feeling rating before: 4 out of 5 frustrated. After: 2. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Gentle stretch rules.","caption":"Stop if it hurts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stretch</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hold, no bouncing</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">if it hurts or you are dizzy</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about belly breathing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Here is a more helpful example: Example: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you slide up a finger and out slowly as you slide down. After one hand, do three slow backwards shoulder rolls. Feeling rating before: 4 out of 5 frustrated. After: 2.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSteps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about belly breathing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSlow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Calming strategies: breathing and stretching","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Feelings and wellbeing","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you slide up a finger and out slowly as you slide down. After one hand, do three slow backwards shoulder rolls. Feeling rating before: 4 out of 5 frustrated. After: 2.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Slow belly breathing in and out.","caption":"Slow and steady.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">In</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">belly rises, slow nose breath</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">slow breath through mouth</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Gentle stretch rules.","caption":"Stop if it hurts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stretch</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hold, no bouncing</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">if it hurts or you are dizzy</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w02-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
